N={}
N[(1,0)]=[
("Who and why","Twenty years after their first review in the same journal, Ryan and Deci look back at what two decades of research on intrinsic and extrinsic motivation have taught us, mostly within <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"sdt\">self-determination theory (SDT)</b>: a broad theory of human development and wellness with strong implications for education."),
("Their starting point","Behaviourist approaches try to shape motivation from outside, with rewards and punishments. SDT starts from the other side: people's <b>inherent</b> tendency to learn and grow, and how to support it. The authors call this part of a \"Copernican turn\"; our reading of the image is that the centre moves from the environment to the learner."),
("Growth needs support","SDT is an <b>organismic</b> theory: people are by nature inclined to grow, master things and connect with others. But these tendencies are not automatic; they need supportive conditions. <i>In plain words:</i> like a plant, a learner grows by herself, but only with the right soil, light and water."),
("What kind of text this is","A review by the theory's founders: nobody knows the evidence better, and it covers hundreds of studies. But it is also an argued case: the authors openly oppose certain policies. Read it like a strong closing statement: real evidence, arranged to convince."),
]
N[(2,0)]=[
("The three needs","Healthy development needs support for three <b>basic psychological needs</b>. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"autonomy\">Autonomy</b>: feeling that your actions are your own. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"competence\">Competence</b>: feeling you can succeed and grow. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"related\">Relatedness</b>: feeling you belong. Frustrating any of them damages motivation and wellness, so SDT judges a school by one question: does it meet or frustrate these needs?"),
("The claim","Two halves. Need support increases intrinsic motivation and internalization, which leads to higher achievement. And, paradoxically, trying to control achievement directly with rewards, sanctions and evaluations generally <b>backfires</b>, producing lower-quality motivation and performance. <i>In plain words:</i> pushing for results from outside tends to get you worse results."),
("What feeds each need","<b>Autonomy</b> grows from interest and value and shrinks under external control, by rewards or punishments. <b>Competence</b> grows in well-structured settings with optimal challenge, positive feedback and room to grow. <b>Relatedness</b> grows when respect and care are shown. Note: structure feeds competence, so SDT is not against structure."),
("What matters most","For the authors, students' growth and wellness matter even more than achievement. Not every student can excel at school's cognitive agenda, but every school can support development and, above all, do no harm. The article then covers types of motivation, classroom evidence, wellness and culture, grades and testing, and teachers' own needs."),
]
N[(3,0)]=[
("The idea","Reading a novel because you love it is <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"intrinsic\">intrinsic motivation</b>: doing something for its own interest and enjoyment. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"extrinsic\">Extrinsic motivation</b> is doing it for any other reason. But extrinsic motivation is a mixed bag, so SDT splits it into four types by how autonomous they are, plus <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"amotiv\">amotivation</b>: no motivation at all."),
("The four extrinsic types","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"extreg\">External regulation:</b> for rewards or to avoid punishment. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"introj\">Introjected:</b> half-internalized; you reward yourself with pride and punish yourself with guilt or shame. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"identreg\">Identified:</b> you consciously value the activity. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"integreg\">Integrated:</b> that value fits your other core values. The first two are <b>controlled</b>, the last two <b>autonomous</b>. Amotivation comes from feeling incompetent, or from seeing no value or interest."),
("The evidence","Research confirms that the types are ordered as the model predicts, and that the more autonomous the motivation, for learning or for teaching, the better the outcomes. Amotivation strongly predicts poorer engagement, learning and wellness."),
("Two refinements","Autonomous extrinsic motivation is not intrinsic motivation: both feel willing, but intrinsic rests on enjoyment, identified and integrated rest on <b>value</b>. And people are not boxes: most behaviour has several motives, so researchers use a <b>relative autonomy</b> score, autonomous and controlled summary scores, or profiles. The authors think intrinsic motivation drives most human learning across life."),
("So what","The useful question is not \"is she intrinsically motivated?\" (school cannot always be fun), but whether her reasons are autonomous or controlled. Teachers can help a reason move toward autonomy. How, and with what results, comes next."),
]
N[(4,0)]=[
("The idea","Two core hypotheses: <b>(a)</b> more autonomous motivation improves engagement, learning and wellness; <b>(b)</b> need support from teachers and parents builds such motivation, and need thwarting undermines it. The authors report that both are well supported across hundreds of studies, ages, subjects and cultures."),
("Evidence for (a)","A meta-analysis found a significant role of intrinsic motivation in achievement (Taylor et al., 2014); intrinsic motivation predicted engagement and through it achievement (Froiland &amp; Worrell, 2016); in college, autonomous motivation predicted performance beyond prior achievement (Manganelli et al., 2019). Likely reasons: more effort, and motivation becoming part of identity, such as seeing yourself as a scientist (Skinner et al., 2017). The worry: intrinsic motivation for school tends to decline over the years."),
("Evidence for (b)","Students of autonomy-supportive teachers show more intrinsic motivation, competence and self-esteem, better grades, more internalization and less dropout. Perceived autonomy support raised engagement through autonomous motivation (Núñez &amp; León, 2019). Law students with autonomy-supportive teachers lost less well-being and did better on the bar exam (Sheldon &amp; Krieger, 2007); mentors' autonomy support predicted medical students' choice of residency."),
("In the body and brain","Controlling teaching raised students' cortisol, a stress hormone, and autonomy-supportive teaching lowered it (Reeve &amp; Tseng, 2011). Brain research suggests choice and optimal challenge may activate reward areas linked to enjoyment and frontal areas needed for engaged learning (Reeve &amp; Lee, 2018); the authors expect this line to grow."),
("So what","Both links of the chain hold. So the practical question moves from the student to the teacher: what exactly does a need-supportive teacher do?"),
]
N[(5,0)]=[
("The idea","A good sports coach is clear about the plan and also listens to the players. A need-supportive classroom rests on two things: <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"autsup\">autonomy support</b> (which feeds autonomy and relatedness) and <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"structure\">structure</b> (which, together with autonomy support, feeds competence). Their opposites are control and chaos."),
("Autonomy support","Autonomy-supportive teachers first try to understand students' perspectives, then offer ownership, meaningful choices, interesting tasks, and a reason when something must be done. Choice needs care: it can be meaningless or come with hidden pressure, and a student can feel autonomous without options if she accepts the task's value. A meta-analysis supports a positive effect of choice on intrinsic motivation (Patall et al., 2008)."),
("Structure","SDT separates structure from control. Control pressures students; structure gives clear expectations and goals, consistent rules, support and rich feedback. Structure itself can be delivered in a controlling or an autonomy-supportive way. Observed autonomy support and structure together predicted engagement in high schools (Jang et al., 2010), and the same pair in parents predicted children's competence and autonomy (Farkas &amp; Grolnick, 2010)."),
("The evidence","<b>Reeve and Jang (2006):</b> eight teacher behaviours, such as listening, time for independent work, encouraging effort and acknowledging students' views, went with autonomous motivation; giving directives and telling the answers went against it. High autonomy support with high structure goes with more autonomous motivation, more self-regulated learning and less anxiety. Aelterman et al. (2019) mapped teaching styles on a <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"circum\">circumplex</b> of eight areas."),
("So what","Need-supportive teaching is neither soft nor strict: it is clear and it listens. Teachers who support autonomy usually support competence and relatedness too, because they are tuned in to students. Next: wellness, for every kind of student."),
]
N[(6,0)]=[
("The idea","For the authors, schools are above all places where young people develop. Confidence, self-esteem and mental health all depend on whether school supports or frustrates the three needs, and they claim this holds across ages, ethnic groups and cultures."),
("The evidence","Need satisfaction went with school satisfaction and well-being in Chinese adolescents; teacher autonomy support predicted engagement, linked to less anxiety and depression; parents' autonomy support helped Finnish students through school transitions. A meta-analysis found autonomy equally important for well-being in East Asian and North American samples (Yu, Levesque-Bristol &amp; Maeda, 2018)."),
("The culture debate","Critics argue autonomy matters less in collectivist cultures (Markus, Kitayama &amp; Heiman, 1996). The authors agree that cultures value autonomy differently, but argue that its <b>function</b> is universal, citing studies from the USA, Russia, Nigeria, India, Japan, South Korea and China. SDT's claims about needs are <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"etic\">etic</b> (universal), while their expression is <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"emic\">emic</b> (culture-specific): the same teacher behaviour can feel more or less controlling in different cultures. In Japan, more autonomously motivated pupils studied more deeply and felt better (Hayamizu, 1997)."),
("Diversity","Autonomy support means trying to understand the learner's own point of view, so it is central to cultural competence and to respecting all differences: background, temperament, interests, religion, sexual identity, ways of processing. With it, students who might feel alienated can feel safer, and a whole school can become more tolerant (Assor et al., 2009)."),
("So what","For the authors, wellness is the main point, and engagement comes with it. That makes the next question uncomfortable: what about the controlling tools every school uses?"),
]
N[(7,0)]=[
("The idea","School policies matter too, sometimes in unintended ways. The authors single out <b>grades</b>, <b>performance goals</b> and <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"hst\">high-stakes tests</b>. Their test for each is <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"funcsig\">functional significance</b>: is it experienced as information (\"here is how you are doing\") or as control (\"perform, or else\")?"),
("Grades","Grades are usually comparative and say little about competence, so used as motivators they are typically experienced as controlling. <b>Grolnick and Ryan (1987):</b> grading lowered intrinsic motivation and conceptual learning. <b>Klapp (2015):</b> in a Swedish natural experiment, grading in primary school was linked to lower later achievement and lower odds of finishing secondary school, especially for lower-ability pupils."),
("Performance goals","Emphasis on grades triggers performance goals (beating others). Performance-avoidance goals appear the most harmful. SDT's reading: performance goals are often experienced as pressure, mastery goals as information. And the <b>reasons</b> behind a goal predicted outcomes better than the goal itself (Vansteenkiste et al., 2010; Gillet et al., 2015)."),
("High-stakes tests","Policies attached rewards and sanctions to test scores, assuming poor results come from poorly motivated teachers. The authors call this remarkably ineffective: teaching to the test, weak students kept away from testing, false reporting, enriching subjects cut, and worse teaching especially for disadvantaged students and English learners. Higher scores did not carry over to other tests. Their principle: rewarding outcomes rewards any route to them. They favour reforms that support the needs of teachers and students."),
("So what","One argument applied three times: a tool experienced as pressure toward an outcome undermines the autonomous motivation that produces the outcome. Much of that pressure comes from above the classroom, which is where the article goes next."),
]
N[(8,0)]=[
("The idea","Teachers have the same three needs as students. SDT holds that teachers can only support students' needs when their own needs are supported. Time, resources and curricula already make autonomy-supportive teaching hard."),
("Pressure from above and below","<b>Pelletier et al. (2002):</b> pressure from above (accountability, controlling administrators) and from below (disengaged students, difficult parents) went with less autonomous motivation in teachers and less autonomy support for students. Work overload and disruptive students lowered teachers' motivation and sense of competence, leading to exhaustion and burnout through frustrated needs. Frustrated teachers become more controlling, and the same pattern holds for principals."),
("Training works","SDT interventions target teachers as the closest influence on students. Across nineteen studies, training in autonomy-supportive teaching had a large effect, 0.63 (Su &amp; Reeve, 2011). Trained teachers were observed to be more autonomy supportive and their students more engaged (Reeve et al., 2004), and the teachers themselves felt more effective (Cheon et al., 2018). Such studies show the causal role of need support and the practical value of the research."),
("So what","Why don't teachers just do this? Because their own needs are under pressure. Real reform supports the needs of teachers and principals, not only their behaviour."),
]

